---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";

import { SOCIALS } from "@/constants";
import { CLOSER, CLOSER_ASIDE, INTRO, TAGLINE } from "@/intro";
import { LAST_TOUCHED, formatStamp } from "@/last-touched";
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";

const projects = (await getCollection("projects")).sort(
  (a, b) => b.data.date.getTime() - a.data.date.getTime(),
);

// Every entry, forever — no pagination, no filtering (ticket 07). The volume is
// the argument.
const SECTIONS = [
  { id: "things", entries: projects.filter((p) => p.data.kind === "thing") },
  { id: "toys", entries: projects.filter((p) => p.data.kind === "toy") },
];

// One link per entry, and it goes to the built thing. The repo used to sit
// beside it as a second link; jass cut it — "let the site choose whether it
// wants to show the person to github". Two entries have no site, so those
// point at the repo and the rule still reads as "the name links to the thing".
const destination = (p: (typeof projects)[number]) =>
  p.data.link ?? p.data.github;

// Tiny machine headings — the one part of stage one jass kept without notes.
const HEADING = "font-machine text-micro text-muted-foreground";
// The scroll margin is the page's own top padding, repeated. Both sections are
// anchor targets (`#things`, `#toys`), and without it a jump welds the heading
// to the top edge of the viewport. Matching `py-12 sm:py-16` means an anchored
// section lands with exactly the air above it that the top of the page has — so
// arriving at `/#toys` looks like arriving at a page, not like a scroll that
// overshot.
//
// These two numbers are one decision in two places. Change the padding and this
// has to follow, or the anchor quietly stops agreeing with the page.
const SECTION = "mt-14 scroll-mt-12 sm:scroll-mt-16";

// The gap between entries was never the problem — it was already the smaller
// number. `text-body` carries a line-height of 1.7, which is a value chosen for
// reading a paragraph, and these rows are not paragraphs. At 390px nearly every
// description wraps to two lines, and 1.7 put 12.6px between those two lines
// against only 8px between one entry and the next. The list was spaced so that
// a single entry looked further apart than two different ones.
//
// So the leading comes down and the gap stays. The rule that has to hold is
// ordering, not size: the space between entries must stay visibly larger than
// the space inside one. At 1.4 that is 8px against 7.2px, and the row is 14%
// shorter into the bargain, which is the part that reads as "less spacing".
//
// Do not tighten the gap below the internal leading to save more height. That
// trade buys pixels and sells the list's structure.
const LIST = "mt-3 space-y-2 text-body leading-[1.4]";

// The page is three things in this order: the intro, things, toys.
//
// There is no work section, and that is a decision rather than an omission.
// Of 71 sites in the research directory, not one puts employment history on
// its index — Anthony Fu, Robby Leonardi, Philippe Dubost and the rest all
// move it to a separate résumé artifact. It kept losing here because it was a
// CV block sitting in a project index, and no styling fixes that. The résumé
// PDF is linked in the socials row and carries it.
//
// jass's reason, which is the better one: where he works and how long he has
// worked shouldn't be what defines him. What he built should.
//
// The intro is jass's, verbatim, and it lives in `src/intro.ts` — three things
// quote it now, so it has one home. Read the comment there before touching it.
---

<Layout title="jass.gg" description={TAGLINE}>
  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-2xl px-6 py-12 sm:py-16">
    <header>
      {
        /* The wordmark is the one place a personal site earns its accent: a
      name in a hand font is a signature, and colouring it is the smallest
      possible way to say the palette out loud. */
      }
      <h1 class="font-hand text-display text-accent">jass</h1>

      {
        /* Measured: at the full 624px column this paragraph sets ~81 characters
      to the line, and comfortable is 60–75. Long measure is the most common
      reason a page of plain text feels faintly tiring to read, and nobody can
      ever name it — the eye loses the start of the next line on the return
      sweep. Capping the prose at 34rem brings it to ~71.

      The cap is on the PROSE ONLY and the lists keep the full width, which is
      not an inconsistency but the point: a paragraph is read once, left to
      right, and suffers from a long measure. A list is scanned down its left
      edge and does not. Narrowing the container instead would fix this and make
      the longest entry wrap on desktop, which is a worse trade. */
      }
      <p class="mt-8 max-w-[34rem] text-body">{INTRO}</p>

      {
        /* The closer, and the whole reason the socials row below it stops being
      an unlabelled list of six words. See `src/intro.ts` before touching it.

      ONE SENTENCE AND ITS MURMUR, on one line. The aside was its own paragraph
      first, which made it a second statement; inline at 11px against 18px it
      becomes what it always wanted to be — an actual aside, said quieter, in
      the same breath. The size gap is doing the work the line break used to do,
      and doing it better.

      `whitespace-nowrap` because the pair does not fit one line at 390px and
      never will. Left alone it breaks mid-phrase and reads as an accident; held
      together it drops whole onto a second line, which is exactly how it looked
      as its own paragraph. Desktop gets the single line, mobile loses nothing.

      accent-quiet, not accent: this page's one full-strength accent belongs to
      the wordmark. `/404` runs the same shape at full accent because it has no
      wordmark to compete with, and the comment there has always said so — this
      is the line it was describing. */
      }
      <p class="mt-5 text-body">
        {CLOSER}{" "}
        <span class="font-hand text-micro whitespace-nowrap text-accent-quiet"
          >{CLOSER_ASIDE}</span
        >
      </p>

      {
        /* These links are 13px tall. Fine to read, mean to hit with a thumb.
      `inline-block` plus vertical padding grows the tap target to ~29px
      without moving a pixel of text, and the negative margin takes the added
      height back out of the layout. Nobody sees this one. That is the point
      of it. */
      }
      <ul
        class="-my-1 mt-7 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 font-machine text-meta text-muted-foreground">
        {
          /* `rel="me"` is the IndieWeb's identity handshake, and it is the
        /llms.txt move again: serve a machine convention properly where no
        human ever looks. The five external links are the claim "these
        profiles are me"; github already links back to jass.gg, which is the
        other half of the proof, and anything that verifies rel=me — Mastodon,
        IndieAuth — gets a real answer. The résumé doesn't carry it because a
        document is not an identity. Zero rendered pixels. */
        }
        {
          SOCIALS.map((social) => (
            <li>
              <a
                class="inline-block py-1"
                href={social.href}
                rel={social.href.startsWith("/") ? undefined : "me"}>
                {social.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </header>

    {
      SECTIONS.map((section) => (
        <section id={section.id} class={SECTION}>
          <h2 class={HEADING}>{section.id}</h2>
          <ul class={LIST}>
            {section.entries.map((entry) => (
              // No separator between name and line: foreground against muted
              // does that work. A dash here collided with the dashes inside
              // the descriptions themselves.
              //
              // That contrast is also what the row hover borrows. Hovering the
              // name lifts the description out of muted too, so the entry
              // wakes as one thing — a name and its line — which is what it is
              // and which nothing at rest says.
              //
              // Keyed off `a:hover` rather than the row's own hover on
              // purpose: pointing at the description does nothing. Text that
              // brightens under the cursor promises a click, and the
              // description isn't clickable. Only the link speaks for the row.
              <li class="group">
                <a href={destination(entry)}>{entry.data.name}</a>
                <span class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground transition-colors duration-150 group-has-[a:hover]:text-foreground">
                  {entry.data.description}
                </span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      ))
    }

    {
      /* THERE IS NO WRITING SECTION. It held one line — `git push -f origin
    twitter`, with the branch name linking out — and jass cut it: add it back
    when there is actually something to add.

    Keeping it was the weaker call and it is worth writing down why, because
    the line was good and a future session will be tempted to restore it. A
    section heading is a promise that something is under it. "writing" with a
    joke where the writing should be is a section admitting it is empty, in a
    list of two sections that are full. The joke was funny; the shape it sat in
    was a placeholder, and this site does not ship placeholders.

    It comes back with a post, not before. `src/data/blog/` still has its
    `draft: true` stub and the collection is untouched. */
    }

    {
      /* The stamp links to the commit it is claiming. A date on its own is an
    assertion; a date that hands you the diff is evidence — and it is the only
    thing on the page that is true because of how the page was built. */
    }
    <footer class="group mt-20 font-machine text-micro text-muted-foreground">
      {
        /* `<time datetime>` because the stamp is a date and the markup should
      say so, not just the pixels — the machine role, carried into the HTML
      itself. The full ISO instant rather than a bare day: the commit knows
      the moment, and truncating it to a date would re-open the timezone
      question the rendered text already glosses. Found only by whoever reads
      source, which is who it is for. */
      }
      {
        LAST_TOUCHED.commitUrl ? (
          <a class="inline-block py-1.5" href={LAST_TOUCHED.commitUrl}>
            last touched{" "}
            <time datetime={LAST_TOUCHED.date.toISOString()}>
              {formatStamp(LAST_TOUCHED.date)}
            </time>
          </a>
        ) : (
          <span>
            last touched{" "}
            <time datetime={LAST_TOUCHED.date.toISOString()}>
              {formatStamp(LAST_TOUCHED.date)}
            </time>
          </span>
        )
      }

      {
        /* The stamp says a date and hands over a link. This says what actually
      changed — the stamp's own argument was that a date alone is an assertion
      and a date that gives you the diff is evidence, and until now it was one
      click short of its own claim.

      Hidden until the footer is hovered, because a commit subject is written
      for whoever works on the repo rather than for a visitor: at rest it is
      noise, found by someone who went looking it is the page showing its
      working. That also makes it the only thing here nobody needs to see.

      `opacity`, not `hidden`, so the space is reserved and nothing moves when
      it appears — and it sits at the very bottom of the page, so even that
      costs nothing above it. `aria-hidden` because a screen reader would
      otherwise read a decoration it can never trigger; the link above already
      says everything that matters.

      `group-focus-within` because hover-only would contradict the site's own
      focus ring, whose comment calls the keyboard the one way to find links
      without a pointer. Tab to the stamp and the working appears; the
      keyboard's hover is focus. Paper gets it too — see the print block in
      global.css — because paper can't hover either, and an invisible
      paragraph would otherwise reserve a blank hole at the end of the
      sheet. */
      }
      {
        LAST_TOUCHED.subject && (
          <p
            aria-hidden="true"
            class="max-w-[34rem] opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-focus-within:opacity-100 group-hover:opacity-100">
            {LAST_TOUCHED.subject}
          </p>
        )
      }
    </footer>
  </main>
</Layout>

<script>
  import { ambient } from "@/scripts/friend";

  /* THROWAWAY (ticket 14). The mechanism needs one act wired to it so the
     grammar can be judged on a real phone: dwell on an entry row and its
     underline waves — draws in, retracts. It borrows the link's existing
     stroke so it adds nothing new to judge except the TIMING, which is the
     only thing on trial. Delete this act the moment the first real demo
     lands; the module stays. */
  for (const row of document.querySelectorAll("section li")) {
    const link = row.querySelector("a");
    if (!link) continue;
    ambient({
      el: row,
      act: () => {
        link.classList.add("friend-wave");
        setTimeout(() => link.classList.remove("friend-wave"), 650);
      },
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* THROWAWAY (ticket 14) — the wave the script above rides. Dies with it. */
  a.friend-wave {
    background-size: 100% 2px;
  }

  /* THE SECTION YOU ASKED FOR SIGNS ITSELF. The page has no nav, so nobody
     arrives at /#things or /#toys from inside the page — an anchor arrival is
     always someone who followed a shared link. When they land, the heading
     draws the same 2px lime stroke a link draws on hover, holds it a beat,
     and retracts it the way it came. Then the page is plain again.

     Derivable, not decorative: the accent appears only when the reader asks
     for it, and following an anchor is asking. The stroke vocabulary already
     belongs to the site — this is the one moment a heading is the thing the
     reader pointed at, so it borrows the pointed-at mark, once.

     `width: fit-content` keeps the stroke under the word; an h2 is a block
     and the gradient would otherwise paint to the column's right edge. It
     changes nothing visible — the text was already at the left edge.

     The 250ms delay lets the jump land before the pen moves, so the draw is
     something the reader sees rather than something that happened during the
     scroll. Costs nothing at rest: the animation ends at background-size 0,
     which is the same state as never having run.

     Scoped style rather than utilities because this needs keyframes, which is
     exactly ticket 09's escape hatch. */
  section:target > h2 {
    width: fit-content;
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      var(--color-accent-mark),
      var(--color-accent-mark)
    );
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    background-position: 0 100%;
    background-size: 0 2px;
    animation: heading-stroke 1.8s ease-out 250ms both;
  }

  @keyframes heading-stroke {
    0% {
      background-size: 0 2px;
    }
    30%,
    70% {
      background-size: 100% 2px;
    }
    100% {
      background-size: 0 2px;
    }
  }

  /* Unlike the link underline, which downgrades to appearing without travel,
     this one is removed outright under reduced motion. The underline is the
     site's only link affordance and must survive; this is a transient
     orientation cue, and scroll position already answers "where am I". A cue
     that exists only as motion goes away when motion does. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    section:target > h2 {
      animation: none;
    }
  }
</style>
